| Condition           | Stationary? |
| ------------------- | ----------- |
| Mean constant       | ✅           |
| Variance constant   | ✅           |
| No trend            | ✅           |
| Trend present       | ❌           |
| Variance changing   | ❌           |
| Sudden level shifts | ❌           |


========================================


A Time Series is Stationary when its mean and variance remain constant over time; otherwise, it is Non-Stationary.

A Stationary Time Series is a series whose statistical properties remain constant over time.

Key Condition:

✅ Mean remains constant

✅ Variance remains constant

✅ No trend

✅ No changing seasonality

========

Mean and Variance is same over the period of time

This means the average value and spread of the data do not change as time progresses.

=============

| Stationary Series    | Non-Stationary Series                |
| -------------------- | ------------------------------------ |
| Mean is constant     | Mean changes over time               |
| Variance is constant | Variance changes over time           |
| No trend             | Upward or downward trend             |
| Easier to model      | Difficult to model directly          |
| Suitable for ARIMA   | Needs transformation before modeling |

============

Example 1: Stationary Series ✅
Daily Temperature Inside an Air-Conditioned Server Room

| Day | Temperature (°C) |
| --- | ---------------- |
| Mon | 22               |
| Tue | 23               |
| Wed | 22               |
| Thu | 24               |
| Fri | 23               |

Mean ≈ 23°C

Variance = Small

The values fluctuate slightly around the same average.

📈 Graph looks like a zig-zag around a fixed level.

Result: Stationary Series

================

Example 2: Non-Stationary Series (Trend) ❌
Company Revenue

| Year | Revenue (Lakhs) |
| ---- | --------------- |
| 2020 | 50              |
| 2021 | 70              |
| 2022 | 95              |
| 2023 | 130             |
| 2024 | 180             |

The average revenue keeps increasing.

📈 Upward trend exists.

Mean is changing over time.

Result: Non-Stationary Series

============

Example 3: Non-Stationary Series (Mean Change) ❌
Employee Salary

| Period  | Average Salary |
| ------- | -------------- |
| Jan-Jun | ₹25,000        |
| Jul-Dec | ₹40,000        |

A sudden jump occurs.

The average before and after is different.

Mean changes.

Result: Non-Stationary

========

Example 4: Non-Stationary Series (Variance Change) ❌
Stock Market

| Month | Daily Price Change |
| ----- | ------------------ |
| Jan   | ±2                 |
| Feb   | ±3                 |
| Mar   | ±20                |
| Apr   | ±25                |


Initially, fluctuations are small.

Later, fluctuations become huge.

Variance changes over time.

Result: Non-Stationary

==============

| Condition           | Stationary? |
| ------------------- | ----------- |
| Mean constant       | ✅           |
| Variance constant   | ✅           |
| No trend            | ✅           |
| Trend present       | ❌           |
| Variance changing   | ❌           |
| Sudden level shifts | ❌           |

================

